 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Solid wastes are unwanted solid materials: food remains, paper, plastic bags and bottles, tins, glass, old clothes, broken phones.\nDumped anywhere, they block gutters (floods), breed flies, rats and mosquitoes (cholera, malaria), smell, and pollute soil and water.\nThe waste hierarchy, best first:\n• Avoid: do not create waste (refuse plastic bags, carry a basket).\n• Reduce: use less (buy in bulk, repair things).\n• Reuse: use again (bottles as containers, old clothes as rags).\n• Recycle: make new things from old materials (plastic into pavement tiles, paper into new paper, metal cans).\n• Compost: turn food and plant waste into manure.\n• Incineration: burning at high temperature in an incinerator (hospital waste); it can pollute the air.\nSort waste into biodegradable (rots) and non-biodegradable (plastic, glass, metal).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "w": "banana peels and leftover food"
  },
  "problem": "What is the best way to manage {w} at home?",
  "steps": [
   "They are biodegradable: microorganisms break them down.",
   "In a compost heap they become manure for the garden."
  ],
  "answer": "Composting.",
  "checks": []
 },
